{
  "summary": "Fixes HTTP status code and response formatting for malformed JSON-RPC 2.0 requests. When a request specifies JSON-RPC 2.0 but has malformed contents such as a missing method or invalid parameter type, the RPC server now returns HTTP 200 OK with a JSON-RPC error payload and an `id` of null if omitted, rather than returning an HTTP error status.",
  "problem": "When request parsing fails after detecting JSON-RPC 2.0, error handling assumed that JSON-RPC version detection had also failed. In debug builds, this trips an `Assume()` check in `JSONErrorReply` that crashes the node for authenticated requests; in release builds, it violates the JSON-RPC 2.0 specification by returning an HTTP error status instead of HTTP 200 OK with the error object.",
  "evidence": [
    "PR description: \"On master, the functional test in this PR actually crashes the node because the assumption is codified... httprpc.cpp:46 UniValue JSONErrorReply(...): 'jreq.m_json_version != JSONRPCVersion::V2' check failed.\"",
    "PR description notes that release builds return HTTP 400 with a JSON-RPC 2.0 error body instead of HTTP 200"
  ],
